# Taste engine

Final numeric taste scores are computed in this folder. Do not generate a dish vector with an LLM.

- Combine with `combineRecipeTaste`: recipe-relative loudness for non-spicy (vs peak + avg; traces quiet as `score × √(score/peak)`), linear or p-norm (p=4), linear gain (1.75×). Spicy skips relative loudness and always p-norms. Cap each dimension at the max in-ingredient.
- Unknown ingredients: cache → chemistry leaf (FAO/INFOODS first, then UmamiDB, Phenol-Explorer, Dr. Duke, FooDB, USDA) → Gemini `estimateLeafTaste` if no lab match for that exact name (`source: "llm"`) → nested full recipe search. One Gemini `confirmFoodShortlists` call must accept each source title against the dish context. Match the food, not a keyword collision. A branded USDA product is allowed only when it *is* that ingredient. Juice/paste/oil inherit the flavoring food, not the carrier. UmamiDB wins free glutamate/nucleotides; Phenol-Explorer wins polyphenol bitter; Dr. Duke wins pungents (ppm midpoint). FAO then USDA then FooDB fill remaining classes. Ignore potassium, vitamin C, and protein amino acids. Do not collapse names (thai chili ≠ chili; soft shell crab ≠ crab). Catalog 0–10 is a mouthful. Tiny compound amounts stay tiny scores. Gemini may calibrate dimensions that have evidence, and may add sour/umami when labs omit acids/free glutamate. Do not invent chili heat: ginger, garlic, mustard, and Sichuan peppercorn are 0 spicy. Lemon/lime fruit ≈ 9 sour, juice ≈ 9.5; orange ≈ 7–8 sweet, onion ≈ 0–1 sweet, black pepper ≈ 0.2 spicy (freshly cracked ≈ 0.5), parmesan ≈ 7–8 salty, soy/fish sauce ≈ 8.5–10 salty; bland vegetables stay near 0. A 10 is the most intense culinary form (salt, sugar, Thai chili). Juice/paste/extract score higher than the whole food. Spicy is chili heat (Thai chili = 10). Chili in a spicy dish is the hot pepper, not sweet chili sauce.
- Representative recipe: ingredients in ≥ 50% of recipes; median volume share. Extract tags `in` vs `out`; only `in` scores. Out-only sides get an “Often served with” list under the scores (primary flavors), still listed quieter in Ingredients.
- Search recipes in the origin culture’s language by default. Typed-language mode searches in the user’s query language instead; both modes share one dish cache row.
- A recipe counts only if the live page fetch returned 2xx. URL Context may parse a JS shell after that; 4xx/5xx pages are skipped. Captcha/human-check redirects still count as 2xx; URL Context and stored links use the pre-challenge recipe URL, not the challenge page.
- Shared dish profiles live in Turso `dishes` (running mean, `timesTasted`, last snapshot). LLM matches the query to a row; it does not invent scores. Nested recipe tastes persist as ingredients, not dishes.
- Site-wide taste count lives in Turso `stats` (`taste_count`). Increment only on a successful top-level profile, not on reject/error/Stop.
- Translate extracted ingredient names to English before listing or resolving. Gemini canonicalizes every name against the ingredient catalog (`canonicalizeIngredientNames(names, catalog, culinaryContext)`).
- Classify the query first (dish / ingredient / reject). Ingredient Turso always wins, even if classify says dish. Reuse cache only gates top-level dishes. Brands and gibberish are rejected.
- Resolve every unique listed ingredient (not only the ≥50% representative set).
- Default model: `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`. Hard cases: `gemini-3.6-flash`. IDs in `lib/engine/models.ts`.
- Add measured profiles in Turso (`INSERT OR IGNORE` on normalized name). Existing catalog rows are never overwritten. Unit tests may use `lib/engine/testdata/ingredients.json`; that file is not the live catalog.
- Tests first: `lib/engine/*.test.ts`.
